<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramesseum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century-bce">13th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ramesseum is the mortuary temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>. Its reliefs depict his campaigns, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, and it preserves a version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kadesh">Egyptian–Hittite peace treaty</a>. A fallen colossal statue of Ramesses in its court is thought to have inspired Shelley's poem Ozymandias.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramesseum.jpg" alt="Ramesseum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve F-E-Cameron · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SFEC_AEH_-ThebesNecropolis-2010-RamsesII-021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Ramesseum lies in the Theban Necropolis on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, across from the modern city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, and is the second largest temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, covering about 10 hectares. It was originally called the House of millions of years of Usermaatra-setepenra, the prenomen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. Work began shortly after the start of his reign and continued for 20 years. The temple followed the standard canons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> architecture: two stone pylons, each leading into a courtyard, then a hypostyle hall of 48 columns around the inner sanctuary.</p><p>Its pylons and outer walls carried scenes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>'s victories, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a>, c. 1274 BCE, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> fleeing before him. One block atop the first pylon records the pillaging of a city called Shalem in his eighth year, which may or may not have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the first courtyard stood colossal statues of Ramesses, called Sun of the princes, and of his mother Tuya. The syenite statue of the enthroned king is estimated to have weighed 1,000 tonnes and stood 16 to 19 metres high; it was moved 220 kilometres from its quarry. Thirty-nine of the 48 columns of the hypostyle hall still stand. A smaller temple beside it was dedicated to Tuya and to his chief wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertari">Nefertari</a>.</p><p>The complex held storerooms, granaries, workshops and a royal palace. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> granaries include the oldest true arch still standing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, from about 1300 BCE. Papyri and ostraca of the Third Intermediate Period show that it was also the site of an important scribal school. On the second pylon a king list names 14 ancestors of Ramesses; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> pharaohs are omitted. In the early Christian era the temple was used as a church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The annual flood on the edge of the floodplain gradually undermined the temple's foundations. French engineers with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s expedition of 1798 identified it with the Tomb of Ozymandias described by Diodorus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In 1815 Giovanni Belzoni was hired to remove the torso of the Younger Memnon, a granite statue of Ramesses; the head reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1818, and is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. The excitement that followed led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley">Percy Bysshe Shelley</a> to write his sonnet Ozymandias, in which a fallen statue at the Ramesseum is now seen, since its cartouche gives the throne name that Diodorus rendered as Ozymandias. Jean-François Champollion, who visited in 1829, coined its name.</p><p>A joint French-Egyptian team has explored and restored the site since 1991, finding kitchens, bakeries and supply rooms, and a school where boys were taught to be scribes. Preservation has been made harder by modern farmers using the area for farming and encroaching on the ruins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramesseum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Kadesh, concluded between Hattusili III and Ramesses II around 1259 BCE, is the earliest peace treaty to survive in the versions of both parties: in Akkadian cuneiform at Hattusa and in Egyptian hieroglyphs at Karnak and the <b>Ramesseum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
